# VRAM preset for a 16GB card (e.g. RTX 4060 Ti 16GB, RX 7800 XT).
#
# CONFIDENCE: best-guess extrapolation, NOT measured on real 16GB hardware -
# this session only had a 12GB card to test on. Read this before trusting it
# for a long/unattended run.
#
# How CHUNK_SIZE was derived: stage 1's peak VRAM was measured at three
# chunk_size values on the 12GB card (110 -> 9.27GB, 150 -> 9.71GB,
# 220 -> 12.72GB, all with CPU_OFFLOAD=model/attention_slicing=True). The
# 110->220 span gives ~0.031GB/frame marginal cost; assumed to hold
# similarly under CPU_OFFLOAD=None (measured baseline: chunk_size=110 ->
# ~12.0GB peak with CPU_OFFLOAD=None), applied against a 16GB card's real
# capacity (~17.16GB, i.e. 16*1024^3 bytes, scaled the same way the 12GB
# card's 12.87GB measured total worked out) with a 15% safety margin (this
# card's own only tested config already runs at ~93% utilization with zero
# headroom, so extrapolating that thin a margin to untested hardware felt
# reckless - 15% gives room for the run-to-run VRAM noise this session
# repeatedly measured, +-1-1.5GB on supposedly-identical runs).
# That nonlinearity is a real risk: window_size/window_overlap interact with
# chunk_size in a way that isn't perfectly linear (see conversation history -
# a chunk_size jump from 150->220 cost 4x more VRAM per frame than 110->150
# did), so treat CHUNK_SIZE below as a starting point, not a guarantee.
#
# TILE_NUM (stage 2): DO NOT just lower this for more VRAM headroom - tested
# and measured wrong originally. This GPU's ROCm build has no working flash/
# efficient attention kernel (confirmed empirically), so attention memory -
# and compute - scale as O(tile_area^2), not linearly. inpainting_inference.py
# now falls back to a manual chunked attention (chunked_attention.py) to
# avoid OOMing at tile_num<4, but that fallback is a *capability* fix, not a
# speed one: measured ~9-11x slower per chunk at tile_num=2 vs tile_num=4 on
# the 12GB dev card (one tile even spiked to 285s for a single step - likely
# allocator fragmentation near the VRAM ceiling). More VRAM on THIS class of
# GPU (AMD/ROCm) does not make smaller tile_num worth it - leave it at 4
# regardless of card size, and spend extra VRAM on CHUNK_SIZE/DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE
# instead.
#
# If you're on an NVIDIA/CUDA card instead: flash-attention actually works
# there, chunked_attention.py's fallback will never trigger (it tries the
# real kernel first and only falls back if that fails), and reducing
# TILE_NUM should be a genuine speedup, matching what NVIDIA users report in
# upstream issues (e.g. tile_num=2 fine on a 24GB card) - try TILE_NUM=3 or
# 2 and compare wall-clock against tile_num=4 yourself; we have no NVIDIA
# hardware to have actually measured that.
#
# DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE is a separate, still-unverified guess extrapolated from
# this repo's existing comment that >1 OOMs at 1080p on 12GB.
#
# RECOMMENDED: before a long run, smoke-test with a single oversized chunk -
# e.g. PROCESS_LENGTH=$CHUNK_SIZE (so stage 1 runs exactly one outer chunk)
# and watch peak VRAM (`watch -n1 nvidia-smi` / `rocm-smi --showmeminfo vram`)
# the way this session did - cheap, and tells you in ~2 minutes whether these
# numbers actually fit before committing to a multi-hour episode.
#
# Usage:
#   source presets/16GB.env && ./run_stereo.sh input.mp4

# ---- stage 1 (depth splatting) ----
export CHUNK_SIZE=190       # est. peak ~14.6GB with CPU_OFFLOAD=None (85% of ~17.2GB)
export WINDOW_SIZE=70       # unchanged - validated for the cross-chunk consistency fix
export WINDOW_OVERLAP=25
export CPU_OFFLOAD=None

# ---- stage 2 (inpainting) ----
export TILE_NUM=4           # see note above - do NOT lower this on AMD/ROCm hardware
export DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE=2  # unverified guess
export DECODE_LATENTS_CHUNK_SIZE=2

# ---- resumability (leave this on - it's the safety net for the above being wrong) ----
export RESUME=True
export MAX_RETRIES=3
